@divider num="i–ii" kind="MODULES" title="What It Is/How It Came Together" sub="The library, its four collections, and the thousand years it took to decide what was in it" foot="TEN PAGES"

@page rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS · 1 OF 4" m="INFORMATION; CANON; TANAKH · LXX; I.1"
# What the Bible actually is
lede: A library, not a book. Sixty-six documents in the Protestant collection this companion starts from, written across roughly a thousand years, in three languages, by people who didn't know they were contributing to the same volume, and who sometimes argued with each other.
k: Which Bible this companion uses, and why that needs saying
We start with the sixty-six-book Protestant canon, because it's the Bible in the room where you were raised and the Bible the Empire inherited. It isn't the only one. Jewish tradition holds twenty-four books in three divisions and doesn't arrange them as an Old Testament. Catholic Bibles include the deuterocanonical books. Orthodox collections include those and more, and differ among themselves, and the Ethiopian Orthodox canon is the largest of all. The shape of the collection is itself an argument about what scripture is for.
k: The internal conversations
tabrl:
| The tension | What's actually in dispute |
| Deuteronomy and Job | Deuteronomy ties covenant faithfulness to flourishing. Job gives a righteous man catastrophe and lets his friends make the simple version of that argument until it fails. The dispute is over retribution used as a rule. |
| Ezra and Ruth | Ezra–Nehemiah dissolves foreign marriages as restoration policy. Ruth makes a Moabite woman David's great-grandmother. Two answers to the same question about who belongs. |
| Proverbs and Ecclesiastes | Proverbs teaches that wisdom can be learned and generally pays. Ecclesiastes watches the same world and reports that the pattern doesn't hold. |
| Court and prophet | The court histories tell kingship from inside the palace. The prophets address the same kings from outside, about the poor, the widow, the foreigner and the worker, and also about idolatry, false worship and foreign alliances. |
box: The working lens
INTERPRETATION: The Bible holds many voices, priestly and prophetic, royal and exilic, addressing different crises. Read these as tensions inside one tradition, not as authors calling each other liars. When a character quotes scripture as if it has one settled opinion, that character is making a choice worth writing about.
/end

@page rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · THE CANONS COMPARED · 2 OF 4" m="TANAKH; LXX; TRENT 1546; ETHIOPIAN CANON"
# Four collections
lede: The same core, different edges, a different order, and the order is an argument. Where a collection ends tells you what it thinks the story is about.
tab:
| Collection | Books | How it's arranged | Where it ends |
| Tanakh (Jewish) | 24 | Torah, Prophets (Nevi'im), Writings (Ketuvim). A complete scripture, not an "Old Testament." | Chronicles, with Cyrus's decree to go up and rebuild. The story closes on return. |
| Protestant | 66 | 39 Old Testament books (the Tanakh's books, counted and ordered differently, with the Prophets moved to the end) and 27 New | Malachi, then Revelation. The Hebrew scriptures end on a promise, which frames them as a prologue. |
| Catholic | 73 | Adds the deuterocanonical books: Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, 1–2 Maccabees, and additions to Esther and Daniel | Includes the Maccabean revolt, armed resistance to an empire that outlawed the faith |
| Orthodox | 76 or more | Varies by communion. Greek, Slavonic and Ethiopian traditions differ from each other and from Rome. | No single answer. Canon has never been uniform worldwide. |
corr: It gave the Protestant New Testament twenty-eight books. It has twenty-seven.
k: Four words that aren't synonyms
**Canon** is which books are in. **Translation** is which words render them. **Edition** is which notes, headings and apparatus surround them. **Lectionary** is which passages get read aloud, and when. An empire that can't change the canon can still change the other three, and the lectionary is the cheapest.
empire: What the Empire would do with this page
POSSIBLE: Never print it. A citizen who knows there are four collections knows there was a decision, and a citizen who knows there was a decision asks who made it. The Empire's Bible has to feel like weather, not legislation.
/end
prompt: Which collection is the Empire's official Bible, and what it leaves out on purpose | fill

